ng anymore so naturally over time  our business has diversified and will  continue to diversify  because across this sector no one can  afford to be left behind  heather urex west global news calgary  canada's employers are struggling coming  up good help has been hard to find the  pandemics impact on the job market  we are at the university of alberta  which is on treaty 6 territory and  within the metis homelands of the metis  nation of alberta region 4. edmonton is  on the traditional territory of the cree  blackfoot denny stoney solto and  blackfoot people  we're back in a moment  you may have noticed help wanted signs  in lots of storefronts and restaurants  despite high unemployment some  industries are having trouble hiring  enough workers and gaviola explains the  employment paradox  across the country popular holiday  destinations have looked a little  different this year the pandemic has  forced closures and modifications at  restaurants and stores and a labor  shortage the likes of which employers  have never seen before  quebec's montreal was grappling with  labor shortages before covid but the  pandemic has made it much worse  immigration is at a relative standstill  and many workers are choosing government  support or moving on from the service  industry they may have child care issues  and safety concerns and with students  returning to school in the fall hiring  is expected to become even more of a  challenge so trouble will recruit  internationally they go to france they  go to middle east they go to  latin america and we have great stores  to tell people who are happy to embrace  these jobs  even in alberta which has suffered job  losses in the oil patch the pool of  candidates has shrunk during the  pandemic the business council of alberta  says employers are having trouble  filling positions across the board from  energy to agriculture with labor  shortages most acute in hospitality what  that falls back to is immigration and  both in terms of te